# Web foundations for Roc authors

This primer assumes you can read Roc. HTML expertise is not required.


Rocci serves HTML over local HTTP. Datastar is the browser transport, not a
client framework. Durable state stays on the server.

## Semantic HTML and identity

Use real elements (`button`, `output`, `main`, `form`) so keyboard, zoom, and
assistive tech have something to work with. Datastar morphs elements by `id`.
Give the region you intend to replace a **stable id**. Changing that id
between responses makes patches miss.

## Documents versus fragments

A GET document is a complete `<html>…</html>`. A patch is a fragment: the
element that should replace the current node with the same `id`. Do not wrap
every patch in a full document shell.

```text
Browser  --GET /-->  @get:view              -->  <html>…<div id="counter">…</div>…</html>
Browser  --POST /actions/…--> @post:fragment --> <div id="counter">…</div>
                                         (Datastar morphs #counter in this tab)
```

## HTTP methods and status codes

| Method | Typical Rocci use |
| --- | --- |
| GET | `@get:view`, `@get:fragment`, and `@get:live` |
| POST | `@post:fragment` or `@post:command` mutations |
| PUT, PATCH, DELETE | The corresponding explicit fragment or command declaration |

Success for a document is `200` `text/html`. A Datastar patch is an event
stream of `datastar-patch-elements`. A command is **empty SSE** for Datastar
(HTTP 200 `text/event-stream`, zero events) and `204 No Content` for ordinary
clients. Handler `?` failures become `500`.

## One real handler

The cataloged counter increments SQLite and returns the card fragment:

```rocci
@post:fragment("/actions/counter/increment") = |{ db }| {
    count = increment_count!(db)?
    counterCard({ count: count })
}
```

The button that calls it uses an unquoted Rocci action, not a JS click
handler:

```rocci
<button type="button" data-on:click=@post("/actions/counter/increment")>
    Increment
</button>
```

Complete source: [Counter.rocci](/examples/counter/source/Counter-rocci/).

## SSE and same origin

`@get:live(path)` generates a long-lived GET at its authored path. The preview window and your
browser load the **same** loopback origin. `rocci.toml` rejects binding
`0.0.0.0`. Cross-origin requests need an explicit allowed origin.

Signals (`$count`) are for ephemeral UI, not the domain. Commands do not
return domain JSON; use rendered fragments for visible state.

Continue with [Rocci in five minutes](/docs/five-minutes/).
